{# RFC §synth-5-J-1 — C11 script-engine helpers (lua_State* direct, no abstraction).

   The C++/Rust/Go/Kotlin backends route script execution through an
   IScriptEngine interface (ScriptEngineProvider in C++/Rust/Go,
   ScriptEngineHost in Kotlin) so that a fixture-level test harness can
   swap a mock engine. The C11 backend is AOT-only and bare-metal-bound:
   the embedded target runs Lua 5.4 directly with no allocator hook, no
   futures, no virtual dispatch. So this template emits raw lua C API
   calls inline, and the abstraction layer that the other backends carry
   never appears in the generated `_sm.c`.

   Macros exported:
     - lua_init_engine()         → create lua_State, openlibs, init datamodel
     - lua_destroy_engine()      → close lua_State (idempotent, NULL-safe)
     - lua_assign(loc, expr)     → execute "loc = (transpiled_expr)" chunk
     - lua_eval_guard(cond, var) → set `var` (bool) to truthiness of cond
     - lua_foreach_no_body(...)  → W3C 4.6 foreach with empty body (var decl
                                   + iteration item/index without C body)
     - lua_foreach_body_prologue / _iter / _epilogue → W3C 4.6 foreach with
                                   a C-side body. Prologue caches the array
                                   on lua global `_sce_foreach_arr` and
                                   exposes its length on C local `_sce_n`;
                                   _iter sets item/index for one iteration;
                                   _epilogue clears the cache. Nested foreach
                                   is intentionally left unsupported — the
                                   fixed lua global name would collide; the
                                   first nested fixture forces unique-id work.

   Every macro takes the Lua state pointer as `sm->L`; the calling
   template owns the surrounding block scope and any per-block error
   handling. Decision: jinja2-inline only —
   no helper class, no shared .c file. #}

{% macro lua_init_engine() %}
    /* W3C SCXML 5.10: Open Lua state and register standard libraries. */
    sm->L = luaL_newstate();
    if (sm->L != NULL) {
        luaL_openlibs(sm->L);
        {% if model.needs_dom_helper %}
        /* W3C SCXML B.2: install metatables for the host-side XML DOM
           helper so subsequent `<data>` / `<send><content>` XML payloads
           can construct DOM userdata via `sce_lua_dom_push_object`.
           Mirrors cpp `LuaEngine::initializeSession` registerMetatable
           hook (sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp:385). The helper is
           defined in sce-c-tests/support/lua_dom_binding.c and lives
           outside the C11 runtime — only fixtures flagged NEEDS_DOM
           link against it. */
        sce_lua_dom_register_metatable(sm->L);
        {% endif %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.9 / 4.6: ECMAScript truthiness for transition cond
           and <if cond>. Lua's native truthiness only treats nil/false as
           falsy, but ECMAScript additionally treats 0, NaN, empty string,
           null, and undefined as falsy — `lua_transformer::wrap_truthiness`
           wraps cond expressions in `_scxml_truthy(...)` whenever the
           expression is not already a Lua-comparable boolean (no
           ==/~=/</>/etc), and that helper must exist in the lua state
           before any cond is evaluated. Mirrors the C++ `LuaEngine.cpp`
           lua_pushcfunction registration; we use a Lua-script definition
           via luaL_dostring instead so the C11 backend stays free of
           lua_CFunction trampolines (jinja2-inline only). */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "function _scxml_truthy(v)\n"
            "  if v == nil or v == false then return false end\n"
            "  if type(v) == 'number' then return v ~= 0 and v == v end\n"
            "  if type(v) == 'string' then return #v > 0 end\n"
            "  return true\n"
            "end");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.4 / 4.6 (test457): ECMAScript `instanceof Array`
           predicate for `<foreach array=...>` validation. Mirrors cpp
           `LuaEngine.cpp:463-483` lua_pushcfunction — strict array check
           (table with `__is_array` metatable, OR table with rawlen > 0).
           Empty tables return false here; the foreach prologue separately
           treats any table as a (possibly empty) array per cpp
           `ForeachHelper.h:173-191` empty-table fallback. C11 lowers the
           cfunction to a Lua-script via dostring (no
           lua_CFunction trampolines). */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "function _isArray(v)\n"
            "  if type(v) ~= 'table' then return false end\n"
            "  local mt = getmetatable(v)\n"
            "  if mt and mt.__is_array ~= nil then return true end\n"
            "  return #v > 0\n"
            "end");
        /* W3C SCXML B.2 (test460): ECMAScript `Array.concat` — produces a
           new table (no mutation of inputs), so a foreach body's
           `Var1 = [].concat(Var1, [4])` rebinds Var1 while the foreach
           prologue's `_sce_foreach_arr` keeps referencing the original
           array (shallow-copy iteration semantic per W3C 4.6). Mirrors
           cpp `LuaEngine.cpp:519-539` lua_pushcfunction; C11 lowers via
           Lua-script (jinja2-inline, no lua_CFunction trampolines). */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "function _concat(...)\n"
            "  local result = {}\n"
            "  local n = 0\n"
            "  for _, arg in ipairs({...}) do\n"
            "    if type(arg) == 'table' then\n"
            "      for _, v in ipairs(arg) do\n"
            "        n = n + 1\n"
            "        result[n] = v\n"
            "      end\n"
            "    else\n"
            "      n = n + 1\n"
            "      result[n] = arg\n"
            "    end\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  return result\n"
            "end");
        /* W3C SCXML B.2 (test224): ECMAScript `String.prototype.indexOf` /
           `Array.prototype.indexOf` — returns the 0-based position of the
           needle or `-1` when absent. `lua_transformer::wrap_method_calls`
           rewrites `<obj>.indexOf(<arg>)` into `_indexOf(<obj>, <arg>)`,
           so the helper must exist before any cond reaches the Lua state.
           Mirrors cpp `LuaEngine.cpp:485-517` lua_pushcfunction (string
           branch via `strstr`, array branch via raw-iter equality);
           C11 lowers to `string.find(.., plain=true)` for strings and a
           1-based ipairs walk for arrays (no lua_CFunction
           trampolines). */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "function _indexOf(obj, value)\n"
            "  if type(obj) == 'string' and type(value) == 'string' then\n"
            "    local s = string.find(obj, value, 1, true)\n"
            "    if s then return s - 1 else return -1 end\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  if type(obj) ~= 'table' then return -1 end\n"
            "  for i, v in ipairs(obj) do\n"
            "    if v == value then return i - 1 end\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  return -1\n"
            "end");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.10: `_sessionid` is a read-only system variable bound
           on startup. The C++ runtime carries a per-instance UUID (set via
           `LuaEngine::setupSystemVariables`); the C11 backend is AOT-only
           so we bake a deterministic per-machine value derived from
           `model.name`. The W3C requirement is that `_sessionid` is bound
           (non-nil) and remains the same value through the session — both
           hold for any non-empty deterministic string. Read-only behaviour
           is enforced at codegen time in `state_machine.c.jinja2`'s
           emit_action assign branch (any <assign location> targeting a
           reserved system variable lowers to error.execution rather than
           a luaL_dostring), so the Lua state itself never sees a
           re-binding attempt. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_sessionid = '{{ model.name | escape_c }}_session'");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.10: `_name` is the SCXML element's `name` attribute
           (cpp `LuaEngine.cpp` calls this `sessionName`). Empty string
           when the SCXML omits the attribute — still a valid Lua string
           and therefore "bound" per W3C 5.10's typeof check. Same
           read-only enforcement as _sessionid: the codegen-time assign
           guard rejects <assign location="_name"> before any luaL_dostring
           is invoked. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_name = '{{ model.scxml_name | default('') | escape_c }}'");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.10 + C.1 (test500/501/569): `_ioprocessors` is a
           table of supported I/O processor descriptors keyed by alias.
           Mirrors cpp `LuaEngine::setupSystemVariables`
           (`sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp:1041-1046`): for each entry
           in `ioProcessors` (cpp's `StateMachine.cpp:3107` seeds it as
           `{"scxml"}`), build `_ioprocessors[proc] = {location = proc}`.
           The C11 AOT-only profile mirrors that exact emit shape — the
           literal `"scxml"` is bound non-nil so test500's typeof check
           passes, and indexed access (`_ioprocessors['scxml'].location`)
           returns a truthy string so test569's cond evaluates true.
           Per-processor entries beyond `scxml` land when the first
           additional-IO-processor fixture forces them. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_ioprocessors = {scxml = {location = 'scxml'}}");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.5 + 3.7: donedata stash slot. The final-state entry
           emit in `state_machine.c.jinja2::execute_entry_actions` populates
           `_pending_donedata` (string / number / table) right before
           raising `done.state.<parent>`; the next dequeue's
           `set_current_event` promotes it onto `_event.data` and resets it
           to nil. The slot is initialised here so the lua-side promotion
           chunk can read `_pending_donedata` even when no `<donedata>` ever
           fires (lua undefined globals already evaluate as nil, but an
           explicit init keeps the contract grep-able). Mirrors the cpp
           `EventWithMetadata::data`/`typedData` carry-on-event shape; C11
           swaps the per-event payload for a lua-side stash to avoid widening
           every event_with_meta_t entry with a typed-value union. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L, "_pending_donedata = nil");
        /* W3C SCXML 5.5 + 6.3.1 cross-SM donedata carry: serialise a
           Lua value (the `_pending_donedata` stash populated by the
           child's `<final><donedata>` entry emit) into a Lua-source
           expression string that the parent's `process_event_queues`
           external dequeue (state_machine.c.jinja2:3493-3498) re-loads
           via `_event.data = (<literal>)`. Cpp ships JSON on
           `EventWithMetadata::data` because its event type is
           engine-agnostic across LuaEngine / JSEngine / mock; C11 is
           AOT-only with Lua 5.4 as its sole script engine, so a Lua-source literal
           is the round-trip-free equivalent and produces a typed value
           on the parent side rather than forcing a re-parse.

           Encoding contract:
             - nil           → '' (empty; receiver skips the override)
             - string        → string.format('%q', v) (Lua-source escaped)
             - number/bool   → tostring(v) (Lua-source compatible)
             - table         → '{k1 = lit1, k2 = lit2, ...}' (identifier
                               keys plain, non-ident keys bracketed).
                               Keys sorted to keep the wire form
                               deterministic so byte-equal output across
                               equal-content donedata payloads
                               (committed-tree-sweep §synth-6.2.6 contract).
           Both parent and child SMs initialise this helper because
           `lua_init_engine` runs in every SM with `needs_script_engine`;
           the cross-SM bridge `lua_marshal_child_donedata` calls it on
           the child's L and the resulting string lands on the
           `done.invoke.<id>` event's `data[]` field. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "function _sce_donedata_to_lua_literal(v)\n"
            "  if v == nil then return '' end\n"
            "  local t = type(v)\n"
            "  if t == 'string' then return string.format('%q', v) end\n"
            "  if t == 'number' or t == 'boolean' then return tostring(v) end\n"
            "  if t == 'table' then\n"
            "    local parts = {}\n"
            "    local keys = {}\n"
            "    for k in pairs(v) do keys[#keys+1] = k end\n"
            "    table.sort(keys, function(a, b) return tostring(a) < tostring(b) end)\n"
            "    for _, k in ipairs(keys) do\n"
            "      local key_str\n"
            "      if type(k) == 'string' and k:match('^[%a_][%w_]*$') then\n"
            "        key_str = k\n"
            "      else\n"
            "        key_str = '[' .. _sce_donedata_to_lua_literal(k) .. ']'\n"
            "      end\n"
            "      parts[#parts+1] = key_str .. ' = ' .. _sce_donedata_to_lua_literal(v[k])\n"
            "    end\n"
            "    return '{' .. table.concat(parts, ', ') .. '}'\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  return ''\n"
            "end");
        /* W3C SCXML 6.2 + 5.10.1 (.raw): ordered (name, value) capture for
           `<send>`'s `<param>` / `namelist` evaluation. cpp builds
           `std::map<string, vector<string>>` to preserve duplicate keys
           (W3C 6.2 mandate) and serialises via
           `EventDataHelper::buildEventDataJson` for `_event.raw`.
           `_pending_donedata` (the Lua table) cannot hold dup keys, so a
           parallel ordered list captures the eval sequence; the
           `_sce_event_data_json()` builder groups by name and emits the
           same shape cpp surfaces on `Event::dataStr`. The bounded
           string then lands on `event_with_meta_t::data[]` and
           `set_current_event` lifts it onto `_event.raw` for the
           receiving transition / log.
           Pairs reset per <send> arm so successive sends in one onentry
           do not bleed into each other's raw payload. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_sce_send_data_pairs = {}\n"
            "function _sce_event_data_json()\n"
            "  if not _sce_send_data_pairs or #_sce_send_data_pairs == 0 then\n"
            "    return ''\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  local order, groups = {}, {}\n"
            "  for _, p in ipairs(_sce_send_data_pairs) do\n"
            "    local k, v = p[1], p[2]\n"
            "    if groups[k] == nil then groups[k] = {}; table.insert(order, k) end\n"
            "    table.insert(groups[k], tostring(v))\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  local function _q(s)\n"
            "    s = s:gsub('\\\\', '\\\\\\\\'):gsub('\"', '\\\\\"')\n"
            "    return '\"' .. s .. '\"'\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  local parts = {}\n"
            "  for _, k in ipairs(order) do\n"
            "    local vs = groups[k]\n"
            "    local val\n"
            "    if #vs == 1 then\n"
            "      val = _q(vs[1])\n"
            "    else\n"
            "      local arr = {}\n"
            "      for _, v in ipairs(vs) do table.insert(arr, _q(v)) end\n"
            "      val = '[' .. table.concat(arr, ',') .. ']'\n"
            "    end\n"
            "    table.insert(parts, _q(k) .. ':' .. val)\n"
            "  end\n"
            "  return '{' .. table.concat(parts, ',') .. '}'\n"
            "end");
        {% if model.needs_donedata_helper or model.needs_namelist_helper %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.7 + C.1 (test343, test553): donedata `<param>` and
           `<send namelist>` evaluation both need to detect ECMAScript
           ReferenceError semantics — referencing an undeclared identifier
           must raise error.execution per W3C 5.7 / 6.2. Lua's native
           semantics return `nil` for undeclared globals, so we register
           a `_scxml_declared` table mirroring cpp `LuaSessionContext::declaredVars`
           (see `LuaEngine.cpp:99-118`, `isUndeclaredIdentifier`). Both the
           donedata param evaluator and the namelist evaluator inspect
           this set together with `rawget(_G, base)` — same two-source check
           as cpp. The set spans top-level <data> and every state-local
           <datamodel> entry so binding mode (early / late) is irrelevant
           to membership; the lua values are placeholder booleans because
           only the keys are read. Emitted only for fixtures that actually
           use donedata or namelist (analyzer-pinned helper flags), so
           non-using fixtures keep their lua init footprint unchanged. */
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_scxml_declared = {"
            {%- set ns = namespace(first=true) -%}
            {%- for var in model.variables -%}
            {%- if not ns.first %}", "{% endif -%}
            "{{ var.id }} = true"
            {%- set ns.first = false -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- for state_id, state in model.states | dictsort -%}
            {%- if state.datamodel -%}
            {%- for var in state.datamodel -%}
            {%- if not ns.first %}", "{% endif -%}
            "{{ var.id }} = true"
            {%- set ns.first = false -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            "}");
        {% endif %}
        {% for var in model.variables %}
        {% if var.expr %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.3: <data id="{{ var.id }}" expr="{{ var.expr | escape_c }}">.
           On expression failure the data element is left undefined (lua nil)
           and error.execution is queued so the SM can react. memset on the
           SM struct in `_init` zero-initialised both queues before this
           loop runs, so direct enqueue is safe at this point. */
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
            memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
            _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
        }
        {% elif var.content %}
        {% if (var.content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
        /* W3C SCXML B.2: inline `<data>` content begins with `<` →
           construct an XML DOM object via the host-side helper. cpp
           `DataModelInitHelper::initializeVariable` isXML branch
           (sce/src/common/DataModelInitHelper.cpp:103-118) 1:1 mirror —
           cpp's `setVariableAsDOM` lowers to `LuaDOMBinding::pushDOMObject
           + lua_setglobal`, which is what `sce_lua_dom_push_object`
           emits below. */
        sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ var.content | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        {% else %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.3/B.2: <data id="{{ var.id }}"> inline non-XML
           content. cpp `DataModelInitHelper::initializeVariable`
           (sce/src/common/DataModelInitHelper.cpp:80-156) dispatch: try
           eval as expression; on Lua syntax/runtime failure, fall back
           to a whitespace-normalized string. */
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            /* W3C SCXML B.2: eval failure → whitespace-normalized literal.
               cpp `DataModelInitHelper.cpp:142-155` (normalizeWhitespace +
               setVariable). */
            lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ var.content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
            lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        }
        {% endif %}
        {% elif var.src %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.2.2: <data id="{{ var.id }}" src="{{ var.src }}">.
           C11 codegen-time file read (RFC §synth-5-J-1 R3 zero-deps: no runtime
           fopen in sce-c-runtime). Mirrors cpp
           `DataModelInitHelper::initializeVariableFromSrc` (sce/src/common/
           DataModelInitHelper.cpp:159-181), which loads the file and
           dispatches to `initializeVariable` — the C11 codegen reads the
           file via the `read_data_src` filter and threads the content
           through the same isXML / eval / whitespace-fallback dispatch as
           inline content. cmake's c11 fixture path copies the `.txt`
           sidecar alongside the SCXML so the codegen-time read resolves
           under ${SCE_C_GENERATED_DIR}. */
        {%- set _src_content = var.src | read_data_src(model.scxml_base_path) %}
        {% if (_src_content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
        /* W3C SCXML B.2: src= file's first non-WS char is `<` → DOM. */
        sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        {% else %}
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ _src_content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
            lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        }
        {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% if model.binding == 'early' %}
        {# W3C SCXML 5.3 early binding (default per parser.rs:378 unwrap_or("early")):
           state-local <datamodel> variables initialise at startup so that any
           state can read them before its own onentry runs. Mirrors the cpp
           scriptengine_helpers.jinja2 early-binding loop (model.states |
           dictsort + state.datamodel guard). The corresponding late-binding
           emit is in `state_machine.c.jinja2::execute_entry_actions` so that
           the per-state init runs on entry before the onentry blocks
           (cpp `entry_exit_actions.jinja2:79-90` mirror).
           Init-failure error.execution raise mirrors cpp
           `DataModelInitHelper::initializeVariableFromExpr` errorCallback
           (datamodel_macros.jinja2:22-24 lambda → engine.raise). The
           top-level loop above and this state-local loop both lift to the
           rc-checked shape (spec-mirror parity carve-out per
           feedback_spec_mirror_parity.md — reference backend's branch is
           the contract). No current corpus fixture has a state-local <data>
           with an illegal expr (test277 only forces top-level), but cpp
           emits the same lambda raise for both top-level and state-local
           loops, so silent-drop here would be coincidence-match drift. #}
        {% for state_id, state in model.states | dictsort %}
        {% if state.datamodel %}
        {% for var in state.datamodel %}
        {% if var.expr %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.3 (early): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}" expr="{{ var.expr | escape_c }}"> */
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
            memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
            _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
        }
        {% elif var.content %}
        {% if (var.content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
        /* W3C SCXML B.2 (early): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}"> inline XML content → DOM. */
        sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ var.content | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        {% else %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.3/B.2 (early): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}"> inline non-XML content. eval+whitespace-fallback per top-level branch above. */
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ var.content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
            lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        }
        {% endif %}
        {% elif var.src %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.2.2 (early): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}" src="{{ var.src }}"> codegen-time file read. */
        {%- set _src_content = var.src | read_data_src(model.scxml_base_path) %}
        {% if (_src_content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
        sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        {% else %}
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ var.id }} = ({{ _src_content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
            lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
        }
        {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% endif %}
        {% if model.global_scripts %}
        /* W3C SCXML 5.8: top-level <script> bodies execute at document
           load time, after datamodel <data> initialisation and before any
           state machine entry. Mirrors cpp `scriptengine_helpers.jinja2`
           lines 165-179 (model.global_scripts loop): each script's content
           routes through `to_lua_script` (compound assignment / increment /
           array-literal / function-syntax transforms — distinct from the
           expression-only `to_lua_expr` used for <data expr> / <assign expr>
           because a script body can be a multi-statement chunk) and lands
           in a luaL_dostring chunk. Chunk failure (load-time syntax error
           or runtime trap) raises error.execution per W3C 5.10, mirroring
           the cpp `engine.raise(typename Engine::EventWithMetadata(
           Event::Error_execution, errorMsg))` shape. The
           document-rejection path (parse_global_scripts setting
           `model.document_rejected = true` on bad src) short-circuits at
           the codegen entry boundary in `sce_codegen.rs:690-706` so this
           loop only runs for fixtures whose scripts parsed successfully —
           src loading failure never reaches the template. */
        {% for script in model.global_scripts %}
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "{{ script.content | to_lua_script | escape_c }}") != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
            memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
            _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
        }
        {% endfor %}
        {% endif %}
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.3 late binding: state-local <data> variables initialise on
   the *entry* of the owning state, before any onentry markup runs. Called
   from `state_machine.c.jinja2::execute_entry_actions` for each state with
   `state.datamodel and model.binding == 'late'`. Mirrors cpp
   `datamodel_macros.jinja2::initialize_state_datamodel` invoked from
   `entry_exit_actions.jinja2:79-90`.

   First-entry-only vs idempotent re-init: cpp's helper (and the lua-direct
   emit here) re-initialises on every entry. The cpp side has no
   first-entry flag in `DataModelInitHelper.h`, and `executeEntryActions`
   is called every time the state is entered. C11 mirrors that — re-entry
   to a state with a `<datamodel>` re-evaluates the init expressions.

   Init-failure semantics: rc-checked, mirrors cpp
   `DataModelInitHelper::initializeVariableFromExpr` errorCallback
   (datamodel_macros.jinja2:22-24 lambda → engine.raise(Error_execution)).
   Spec-mirror parity carve-out per feedback_spec_mirror_parity.md — the
   reference backend's branch IS the contract, missing it would be silent
   semantic drift / coincidence-match. No current corpus fixture has a
   state-local <data> with an illegal expr that drives binding="late" (only
   test307 has the shape and is a manual test), but cpp emits the same
   lambda raise from both early and late call sites, so the C11 macro
   matches that 1:1 regardless of differential corpus coverage. #}
{% macro lua_init_state_datamodel(state, state_id) %}
    {% for var in state.datamodel %}
    {% if var.expr %}
    /* W3C SCXML 5.3 (late): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}" expr="{{ var.expr | escape_c }}"> */
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
    }
    {% elif var.content %}
    {% if (var.content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
    /* W3C SCXML B.2 (late): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}"> inline XML content → DOM. */
    sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ var.content | escape_c }}");
    lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
    {% else %}
    /* W3C SCXML 5.3/B.2 (late): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}"> inline non-XML content. eval+whitespace-fallback per top-level branch in the early-binding macro above. */
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ var.id }} = ({{ var.content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ var.content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
    }
    {% endif %}
    {% elif var.src %}
    /* W3C SCXML 5.2.2 (late): state '{{ state_id }}' <data id="{{ var.id }}" src="{{ var.src }}"> codegen-time file read. */
    {%- set _src_content = var.src | read_data_src(model.scxml_base_path) %}
    {% if (_src_content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
    sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | escape_c }}");
    lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
    {% else %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ var.id }} = ({{ _src_content | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        lua_pushliteral(sm->L, "{{ _src_content | normalize_ws | escape_c }}");
        lua_setglobal(sm->L, "{{ var.id }}");
    }
    {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro lua_destroy_engine() %}
    if (sm->L != NULL) {
        lua_close(sm->L);
        sm->L = NULL;
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# Emit a bounded block that assigns `loc = (expr)` via luaL_dostring.
   On chunk failure (illegal location, illegal expression, type error)
   the macro pops the lua error message off the stack and enqueues an
   `error.execution` event onto the SM's internal queue — W3C SCXML 5.4
   mandates that an unsuccessful <assign> raise this event so the
   surrounding state's `transition event="error.execution"` can fire.
   The raise emit shape is identical to the <raise> action emit in
   `state_machine.c.jinja2::emit_action`; `_err_evt` is declared inside
   the inner if-block scope so concurrent <assign>s in the same onentry
   block do not collide. The EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION enum value is always
   present whenever this path runs — `analyzer.rs::apply_script_engine_implications`
   injects "error.execution" into model.events for every fixture that
   sets needs_script_engine, and any fixture using <assign> does. The
   guard/eval failure on cond expressions remains silent-drop until a
   negative-cond fixture forces it. #}
{% macro lua_assign(loc, expr) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ loc }} = ({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# In-foreach-body assign variant: same chunk as lua_assign, but the
   luaL_dostring return value is checked and the enclosing C `for` loop
   is broken on failure (W3C SCXML 4.6 — first error inside <foreach>
   body must cease execution of the loop). On error the lua stack carries
   the chunk's error message; pop it so the next caller sees a clean
   stack. The surrounding-block-abort half of the W3C sentence (cease
   "the block that contains it") is deferred until a fixture forces the
   stricter semantics — test156's pass guard only needs loop break.
   This emit path is reachable only when emit_action is invoked with
   `in_foreach=true`; the standalone `lua_assign` path stays silent-drop
   so existing fixtures (test147 outside <foreach>) are unaffected. #}
{% macro lua_assign_in_foreach_body(loc, expr) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ loc }} = ({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        break;
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# Emit a bounded block that evaluates `cond` and stores its truthiness in
   `result_var` (must be `bool` and already declared by the caller). The
   chunk returns the value, lua_toboolean lifts it, lua_pop drops the
   stack slot.

   W3C SCXML 5.9 (test344): "If a conditional expression cannot be
   evaluated as a boolean value or its evaluation causes an error, the
   SCXML processor MUST place the error 'error.execution' in the
   internal event queue." On luaL_dostring failure (syntax error, runtime
   trap, etc.) we pop the error message, raise error.execution, and leave
   `result_var` at its initial false — mirrors cpp `LuaEngine::evaluateBoolean`
   chunk-failure path (LuaEngine.cpp:~880, returns false + sets error
   so the surrounding `process_transition` can route the error to a
   handler). The success path is unchanged from the prior silent-drop
   shape so cond expressions that legitimately evaluate to false (e.g.
   test309's `cond="return"` racing with an unconditional fall-through
   transition) keep producing the same observable: result_var stays
   false, the next transition fires eventlessly before error.execution
   is dequeued, and pass is reached. #}
{% macro lua_eval_guard(cond, result_var) %}
    {{ result_var }} = false;
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "return ({{ cond | to_lua_guard | escape_c }})") == 0) {
        {{ result_var }} = (bool)lua_toboolean(sm->L, -1);
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
    } else {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# Emit a body-empty <foreach>: declare item (and optional index) on the Lua
   datamodel, then iterate through `array` setting item/index each step.
   W3C SCXML 4.6 mandates that even an empty foreach declares its item /
   index variables (so a later state can test them via `typeof`).
   Index is 0-based to mirror cpp `ForeachHelper::setForeachIterationVariables`
   (size_t i passed verbatim). Non-array `array` values: the empty-body
   variant proceeds silently — the no-body case has no observable
   side-effect to raise about (item/index already set to nil), and the
   first force fixture for a body-less foreach over a non-array would
   need a separate raise lift. body-bearing foreach routes to the
   prologue/iter/epilogue trio above (state_machine.c.jinja2 lines
   233-256), never to this macro — the call-site `{% if action.actions %}`
   guard is the single seam. #}
{% macro lua_foreach_no_body(array, item, index) %}
    (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ item }} = nil; "
        {% if index %}"{{ index }} = nil; "{% endif %}
        "do local _a = ({{ array | to_lua_expr | escape_c }}); "
        "if _isArray(_a) or type(_a) == 'table' then "
        "for _i, _v in ipairs(_a) do "
        "{{ item }} = _v"
        {% if index %}"; {{ index }} = _i - 1"{% endif %}
        " end end end");
{% endmacro %}

{# Body-bearing foreach prologue: stash the array on `_sce_foreach_arr` and
   the length on local `_sce_n`. Returns -1 for non-table values; the
   caller's `if (_sce_n < 0) raise + return;` arm raises error.execution
   per W3C 4.6 + 5.4 (mirrors cpp `ForeachHelper::evaluateForeachArray`
   nullopt → caller raise). For tables that pass `_isArray` returns
   `#_sce_foreach_arr`; for empty / dictionary-shaped tables (no integer
   keys, rawlen 0) falls back to length 0 per cpp's empty-table
   fallback at `ForeachHelper.h:173-191`. Caller declares the C-local
   `_sce_n`. #}
{% macro lua_foreach_body_prologue(array, item, index) %}
    int _sce_n = -1;
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "_sce_foreach_arr = ({{ array | to_lua_expr | escape_c }}); "
        "if _isArray(_sce_foreach_arr) then return #_sce_foreach_arr "
        "elseif type(_sce_foreach_arr) == 'table' then return 0 "
        "else return -1 end") == 0) {
        _sce_n = (int)lua_tointeger(sm->L, -1);
    }
    lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
    /* W3C 4.6: declare item / index on the datamodel even for empty arrays. */
    (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "{{ item }} = nil"
        {% if index %}"; {{ index }} = nil"{% endif %});
{% endmacro %}

{# Per-iteration update: set item to `_sce_foreach_arr[_i+1]` (lua is
   1-based, W3C index is 0-based; the C loop counter `_i` is the W3C
   index). Variable names come from the fixture; the snprintf buffer is
   sized for the W3C corpus (≤ 32-char identifiers). On chunk failure
   (e.g. illegal item identifier like `'continue'` triggering a Lua
   syntax error) raise EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION and `break` the C loop —
   W3C 4.6 mandates loop termination on iteration failure. The
   surrounding entry-block continues per the bb1c4503 carve-out
   (W3C 4.6 surrounding-block-abort lifts with its own force fixture). #}
{% macro lua_foreach_body_iter(item, index) %}
    {
        char _sce_buf[96];
        (void)snprintf(_sce_buf, sizeof(_sce_buf),
            "{{ item }} = _sce_foreach_arr[%d]"
            {% if index %}"; {{ index }} = %d"{% endif %},
            _i + 1{% if index %}, _i{% endif %});
        if (luaL_dostring(sm->L, _sce_buf) != 0) {
            lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
            memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
            _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
            {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
            break;
        }
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# Tear down the cached array global so a sibling foreach in the same
   onentry block does not pick up a stale reference. #}
{% macro lua_foreach_body_epilogue() %}
    (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L, "_sce_foreach_arr = nil");
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.5: evaluate `<donedata><content expr="X"/></donedata>` and
   stash the value on `_pending_donedata` so the next dequeue's
   `set_current_event` can promote it onto `_event.data`. On chunk failure
   the lua error is popped, `_pending_donedata` is reset to nil (W3C 5.5:
   "Return empty data when evaluation fails"), and an `error.execution`
   event is queued (W3C 5.10). Mirrors cpp `DoneDataHelper::evaluateContent`
   — the cpp path returns `outEventData = ""` on failure but still emits
   the done.state event; the C11 emit follows that same flow because the
   structural-error short-circuit only applies to params, not content. #}
{% macro lua_eval_donedata_content_expr(expr) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "_pending_donedata = ({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L, "_pending_donedata = nil");
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.10 + 6.2 (test179, test562, test578): best-effort literal
   `<send><content>BODY</content></send>` evaluator. Mirrors cpp
   `EventDataHelper::jsonStringToScriptValue` (`sce/src/common/EventDataHelper.cpp:122-139`)
   — cpp tries JSON parse on bodies that begin with a JSON-eligible
   character (`{ [ " t f n digit -`) and falls back to the raw string
   otherwise. The C11 emit follows the same shape inline:
     1. capture the body as a Lua [[long-string]] (preserves embedded
        whitespace/newlines, no escape gymnastics)
     2. apply a JSON→Lua key-syntax shim (`"key":` → `["key"]=`) so
        a JSON object/array becomes a valid Lua table literal
     3. attempt `load("return (" .. _try .. ")")` + `pcall` — succeeds
        for numeric/boolean/JSON-shape bodies (test179 numeric, test578
        JSON object); the table/value lands on `_pending_donedata`
     4. on parse failure, W3C 5.10 ECMAScript datamodel space-normalize
        the body (collapse `%s+` to single space, trim) and use it as
        the string payload (test562 plain text, JSON-with-syntax-error
        also routes here). No `error.execution` raise — W3C distinguishes
        literal `<content>` (best-effort parse, never fails) from
        `<content expr="...">` (expression eval, raises on failure).
   The body is required not to contain `]]` (would close the long
   string prematurely); the W3C corpus has no such fixture, and the
   first force fixture lifts the level via `[==[...]==]`. #}
{% macro lua_send_content_literal(content) %}
    {% if (content | trim) is startingwith('<') %}
    /* W3C SCXML B.2 (test561): the literal `<content>` body's first
       non-whitespace character is `<` — deliver it as an XML DOM
       object on `_pending_donedata`, which the next dequeue's
       `set_current_event` call promotes onto `_event.data`. Mirrors
       cpp `LuaEngine::setCurrentEvent` first-char check
       (sce/src/scripting/LuaEngine.cpp:1145-1149) — cpp parses event
       data the same way at delivery time; C11 parses at send time and
       carries the userdata through the registry-anchored
       `_pending_donedata` slot, which still produces the correct
       `_event.data` shape because Lua's `_event = {... data=...}`
       construction stores any value type, including DOM userdata. */
    sce_lua_dom_push_object(sm->L, "{{ content | escape_c }}");
    lua_setglobal(sm->L, "_pending_donedata");
    {% else %}
    (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "do "
          "local _c = [[{{ content | escape_c }}]]; "
          "local _try = _c:gsub('\"([^\"]*)\"%s*:', '[\"%1\"]='); "
          "local _fn = load('return (' .. _try .. ')'); "
          "local _ok = false; local _val = nil; "
          "if _fn then _ok, _val = pcall(_fn) end; "
          "if _ok then _pending_donedata = _val "
          "else _pending_donedata = _c:gsub('%s+', ' '):match('^%s*(.-)%s*$') end "
        "end");
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.7: evaluate one `<param name=N expr=X/>` (or location=X) and
   set `_pending_donedata.N = (X)`. The caller emits `_pending_donedata = {}`
   once before iterating so the table exists. On chunk failure W3C 5.7
   says "ignore the failed param, continue with others"; the macro pops
   the lua error and queues `error.execution` without aborting the loop.
   Structural-error (empty location AND empty expr) is decided at codegen
   time by the caller and never reaches this macro. The chosen-expression
   precedence (location > expr) mirrors cpp's
   `param.location if param.location else param.expr`.

   ECMAScript ReferenceError detection (W3C 5.7, test343): cpp
   `LuaEngine::evaluateExpressionInternal` (`LuaEngine.cpp:795`) detects
   undeclared simple variables via `isUndeclaredSimpleVariable` and
   returns `ScriptResult::createError("ReferenceError: ...")`, which
   triggers the param-error `error.execution` raise upstream. C11 mirrors
   that check inside the lua chunk: extract the base identifier of the
   chosen expression (everything up to the first `.` / `[` / operator),
   look it up in `_scxml_declared` (set up in `lua_init_engine` to mirror
   cpp's `declaredVars`) and `rawget(_G, base)` (covers Lua standard
   globals like `math` / `string` and any system var written via
   `_xxx = ...`). Missing on both paths is undeclared → error → upstream
   pcall catches it and the macro raises error.execution. The base
   extraction uses lua's pattern `^[%a_][%w_]*` so multi-segment
   expressions like `Var1.foo` only check the first segment, exactly
   like cpp's `expr.substr(0, baseEnd)`. #}
{% macro lua_eval_donedata_param_expr(param_name, chosen_expr) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "do "
          "local _expr = '{{ chosen_expr | escape_c }}'; "
          "local _base = _expr:match('^[%a_][%w_]*'); "
          "if _base and not _scxml_declared[_base] and rawget(_G, _base) == nil then "
            "error('ReferenceError: ' .. _base .. ' is not defined') "
          "end; "
          "_pending_donedata.{{ param_name }} = ({{ chosen_expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }}) "
        "end") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 6.2 + C.1 (test553): evaluate one whitespace-tokenised
   namelist variable name, store its value on `_pending_donedata.<name>`,
   and raise `error.execution` if the name is not declared in the
   datamodel. Mirrors cpp `NamelistHelper::evaluateNamelist`
   (`sce/include/common/NamelistHelper.h:88-131`) — cpp gates evaluation
   on `hasVariable` (= `LuaSessionContext::declaredVars.contains(name)`)
   and stops dispatch on the first miss. Lua's silent-nil-for-undeclared
   semantic is bridged by the same two-source check the donedata param
   evaluator uses: `_scxml_declared` (set up in `lua_init_engine` for
   donedata + namelist consumers) + `rawget(_G, name)`. The macro
   raises error.execution and `return;` so the surrounding entry/exit/
   transition function aborts subsequent content per W3C 5.10. The
   caller emits `_pending_donedata = {}` once before iterating and
   dispatches the event after the loop completes — falling through
   means every name resolved cleanly. #}
{% macro lua_eval_namelist_var(var_name) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "do "
          "if not _scxml_declared['{{ var_name | escape_c }}'] and "
            "rawget(_G, '{{ var_name | escape_c }}') == nil then "
            "error('namelist variable not declared: {{ var_name | escape_c }}') "
          "end; "
          "_pending_donedata.{{ var_name }} = ({{ var_name }}) "
        "end") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L, "_pending_donedata = nil");
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_event_with_meta_t _err_evt;
        memset(&_err_evt, 0, sizeof(_err_evt));
        _err_evt.event = {{ csym_prefix | upper }}{{ model.name | upper }}_EVENT_ERROR_EXECUTION;
        {{ csym_prefix }}{{ model.name }}_raise(sm, &_err_evt);
        return;
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# Consumer-agnostic to-string evaluator: evaluate an arbitrary lua-side
   expression and copy the resulting `tostring(...)` value into a
   caller-owned char buffer (`out_buf`, capacity `out_cap`). Active
   consumers: `<send eventexpr>` (W3C 6.2), `<send targetexpr>`,
   `<send typeexpr>`, `<log expr>` (W3C 4.4), `<cancel sendidexpr>`
   (W3C 6.3). The chunk pushes `tostring((expr))` onto the lua stack so
   any value type (string / number / bool) coerces to its conventional
   textual form before C reads it — mirrors cpp `ScriptResultUtils::resultToString`
   semantics. On chunk failure the buffer is left empty and `out_ok`
   becomes false; the caller is responsible for raising error.execution
   and stopping the rest of the action per W3C 5.10. lua_pop runs
   unconditionally so the stack is clean for the next caller. Buffer
   cap is per-call-site (64 for short event names / target tokens, 256
   for log payloads / typeexpr URIs); fixtures that exceed a cap raise
   it locally without affecting other consumers. #}
{% macro lua_eval_to_string(expr, out_buf, out_cap, out_ok) %}
    {{ out_ok }} = false;
    {{ out_buf }}[0] = '\0';
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "return tostring(({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }}))") == 0) {
        size_t _evt_len = 0u;
        const char *_evt_str = lua_tolstring(sm->L, -1, &_evt_len);
        if (_evt_str != NULL) {
            if (_evt_len >= (size_t)({{ out_cap }})) {
                _evt_len = (size_t)({{ out_cap }}) - 1u;
            }
            memcpy({{ out_buf }}, _evt_str, _evt_len);
            {{ out_buf }}[_evt_len] = '\0';
            {{ out_ok }} = true;
        }
    }
    lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 6.2 (test175): evaluate `<send delayexpr="...">` and parse the
   resulting string into milliseconds, mirroring cpp
   `SendSchedulingHelper::parseDelayString`. Grammar (parser.rs:2799):
     - "Nms"   → N
     - "Ns"    → N * 1000
     - bare N  → N * 1000   (W3C corpus default)
   Negative results, malformed strings, or non-numeric values yield -1 and
   set `out_ok = false`; the caller raises `error.execution` per W3C 5.10.
   The lua side performs the entire parse so the C side stays free of
   `strtod` / locale concerns and inherits the same number grammar that
   `to_lua_expr`'s expression-eval path would see for the inner value.
   The chunk runs at the chunk top level (not inside a `do`/`function`
   block) so `return` exits the chunk's pcall directly, matching
   `lua_eval_to_string`'s pattern. #}
{% macro lua_eval_delayexpr(expr, out_ms, out_ok) %}
    {{ out_ok }} = false;
    {{ out_ms }} = 0;
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "local _s = tostring(({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }})); "
        "local _n, _u = _s:match('^%s*([%d.]+)%s*(%a*)%s*$'); "
        "if not _n then return -1 end; "
        "local _v = tonumber(_n); "
        "if not _v or _v < 0 then return -1 end; "
        "if _u == 'ms' then return math.floor(_v) "
        "elseif _u == 's' or _u == '' then return math.floor(_v * 1000) "
        "end; "
        "return -1") == 0) {
        lua_Integer _ms = lua_tointeger(sm->L, -1);
        if (_ms >= 0) {
            {{ out_ms }} = (uint64_t)_ms;
            {{ out_ok }} = true;
        }
    }
    lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 6.4 (test226/240/241/243/244/245/276): cross-Lua-state value
   transfer for `<invoke><param>` / `<invoke namelist>`. Parent's
   `execute_pending_invokes` evaluates the expression (for params) or reads
   the global (for namelist) on its own `lua_State` so the value lands on
   `src_L`'s top of stack; this macro copies that top value to `dst_L`'s
   global table under `dst_name` and pops the source slot.

   cpp `invoke_methods.jinja2:118-128` passes the param's expr STRING to the
   child's script engine (`child->setParamInScriptEngine(name, expr)`) so
   the child re-evaluates in its own context. C11 cannot mirror that shape:
   each generated SM owns a dedicated `lua_State` per `_init`, so a string
   that names a parent-side variable would be undefined in the child's
   state. Instead we evaluate parent-side and transfer the value — which is
   also closer to W3C 6.4.1's intent ("evaluate the expr in the calling
   context"). For literal-expression W3C tests (all of test226/240/241/243
   /244/245/276 use `expr="1"` or namelist names that resolve to integers),
   both shapes converge on the same observable child datamodel.

   Type dispatch covers the W3C corpus param/namelist value types: integer
   / float (Lua number, distinguished via lua_isinteger so 0 stays an int
   in the child datamodel), string, boolean. Non-primitives (table /
   function / userdata) collapse to nil — no fixture in the W3C corpus
   passes those; the first one forces a typed-payload helper. nil and
   none transfer as nil. The pop on `src_L` keeps the parent's stack
   balanced for sibling-param iteration. #}
{% macro lua_xfer_top_to_child(src_L, dst_L, dst_name) %}
        {
            int _xfer_t = lua_type({{ src_L }}, -1);
            if (_xfer_t == LUA_TBOOLEAN) {
                lua_pushboolean({{ dst_L }}, lua_toboolean({{ src_L }}, -1));
            } else if (_xfer_t == LUA_TNUMBER) {
                if (lua_isinteger({{ src_L }}, -1)) {
                    lua_pushinteger({{ dst_L }}, lua_tointeger({{ src_L }}, -1));
                } else {
                    lua_pushnumber({{ dst_L }}, lua_tonumber({{ src_L }}, -1));
                }
            } else if (_xfer_t == LUA_TSTRING) {
                size_t _xfer_len = 0;
                const char *_xfer_s = lua_tolstring({{ src_L }}, -1, &_xfer_len);
                if (_xfer_s != NULL) {
                    lua_pushlstring({{ dst_L }}, _xfer_s, _xfer_len);
                } else {
                    lua_pushnil({{ dst_L }});
                }
            } else {
                lua_pushnil({{ dst_L }});
            }
            lua_setglobal({{ dst_L }}, "{{ dst_name }}");
            lua_pop({{ src_L }}, 1);
        }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.10 + 6.2: evaluate one `<send><param name=N expr=X/></send>`
   and store the value as `_pending_donedata.{name} = (X)`. The caller emits
   `_pending_donedata = {}` once before iterating so the table exists, and
   the next dequeue's `set_current_event` promotes the table onto
   `_event.data`.

   Error semantics (W3C 6.2 ≠ 5.7): cpp `actions/send.jinja2` (lines 220-228,
   488-505) on param-expr eval failure logs and pushes empty string — it does
   NOT raise `error.execution`. This is the opposite of donedata's W3C 5.7
   path (`lua_eval_donedata_param_expr`) which raises on failure. The C11
   macro mirrors cpp send: silent-drop on chunk failure, set the slot to
   empty string so subsequent reader code sees a defined-but-empty value.

   Slot reuse: `_pending_donedata` is shared with the donedata path. Within
   the same microstep, a state's `<send>` with params and that state's
   `<donedata>` would last-write-wins on this slot, but the W3C corpus has
   no fixture forcing both in one onentry (`done.state.<parent>` is raised
   from a final state, not from a state that also issues `<send>`). The
   first such fixture lifts the slot to a per-event table keyed on
   `evt.send_id` (cpp's `EventWithMetadata::eventData` per-event carry
   equivalent). Documented at the call site as well so the seam is grep-able.

   No `_scxml_declared` ReferenceError check: cpp send does not invoke
   `LuaEngine::isUndeclaredSimpleVariable` (`LuaEngine.cpp:99`) on send
   params (only donedata params per W3C 5.7's structural-error semantics);
   the macro stays free of that lookup so fixtures without `<donedata>` do
   not need to declare `_scxml_declared`. #}
{% macro lua_eval_send_param_expr(param_name, expr) %}
    if (luaL_dostring(sm->L,
        "do local _v = ({{ expr | to_lua_expr | escape_c }}); "
        "_pending_donedata.{{ param_name }} = _v; "
        "table.insert(_sce_send_data_pairs, {'{{ param_name }}', _v}) end") != 0) {
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
        (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L,
            "_pending_donedata.{{ param_name }} = ''; "
            "table.insert(_sce_send_data_pairs, {'{{ param_name }}', ''})");
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 6.2 + 5.10.1 (.raw): marshal the param/namelist eval pairs
   captured in `_sce_send_data_pairs` (see `lua_eval_send_param_expr` /
   `lua_eval_namelist_var`) into the JSON shape cpp surfaces on
   `Event::dataStr`, then copy the bounded string onto the event's
   `data[]` buffer. The carry survives `event_queue_push` (struct-copy)
   and `set_current_event` lifts it onto `_event.raw` on dequeue. The
   buffer-overflow guard truncates to `SCE_MAX_DATA_LEN - 1`; the W3C
   manual-test corpus (test178) fits well under that bound. #}
{% macro lua_marshal_send_event_data(send_evt_var) %}
    (void)luaL_dostring(sm->L, "_pending_event_data_json = _sce_event_data_json()");
    {
        size_t _raw_n = 0;
        lua_getglobal(sm->L, "_pending_event_data_json");
        if (lua_isstring(sm->L, -1)) {
            const char *_raw = lua_tostring(sm->L, -1);
            _raw_n = strlen(_raw);
            if (_raw_n >= sizeof(({{ send_evt_var }}).data)) {
                _raw_n = sizeof(({{ send_evt_var }}).data) - 1;
            }
            memcpy(({{ send_evt_var }}).data, _raw, _raw_n);
        }
        ({{ send_evt_var }}).data[_raw_n] = '\0';
        lua_pop(sm->L, 1);
    }
{% endmacro %}

{# W3C SCXML 5.5 + 6.3.1 cross-SM donedata carry (parent ← child).

   Called from `invoke_methods.jinja2` immediately before
   `_raise_external(sm, &_done_evt)` at every site that synthesises a
   `done.invoke.<id>` event for a finished invoked child. The macro
   reads the child SM's `_pending_donedata` lua global (the stash
   populated by the child's `<final><donedata>` entry emit in
   `state_machine.c.jinja2::execute_entry_actions`), serialises it to a
   Lua-source expression via `_sce_donedata_to_lua_literal`, and copies
   the resulting bounded string onto `_done_evt.data[]`.

   The parent's `process_event_queues` external dequeue
   (state_machine.c.jinja2:3493-3498) sees a non-empty `evt.data` and
   evaluates `_event.data = (<literal>)` on its own `sm->L`, producing
   a typed lua value the parent's `done.invoke.<id>` transition cond
   can dereference (`_event.data.result === 42`, `_event.data ===
   'hello_content'`, etc.). Empty `_pending_donedata` (child final
   without `<donedata>`) serialises to '' → `_done_evt.data` zero-init
   stays → receiver skips the override (`_event.data` remains nil),
   matching W3C 5.10 "no donedata → undefined" contract for non-
   donedata invokes (test247-shape children).

   This bridge mirrors cpp `donedataAtFinal()` + `typedDonedataAtFinal()`
   carried on `EventMetadataHelper::createDoneInvokeEvent`
   (invoke_methods.jinja2:189-195) — cpp ships JSON because
   `EventWithMetadata` crosses engine-agnostic boundaries; C11's Lua-
   source carry is the round-trip-free equivalent under the AOT-only +
   Lua 5.4-only engine contract.

   Caller must gate on `invoke_info.child_needs_script_engine` so the
   child has a valid `L` to query — children without a script engine
   never run donedata-expression evaluation, so their carry would be
   empty anyway. #}
{% macro lua_marshal_child_donedata(child_L_var, evt_var) %}
    {
        (void)luaL_dostring({{ child_L_var }},
            "_sce_donedata_literal = _sce_donedata_to_lua_literal(_pending_donedata)");
        lua_getglobal({{ child_L_var }}, "_sce_donedata_literal");
        if (lua_isstring({{ child_L_var }}, -1)) {
            const char *_lit = lua_tostring({{ child_L_var }}, -1);
            size_t _lit_n = strlen(_lit);
            if (_lit_n >= sizeof(({{ evt_var }}).data)) {
                _lit_n = sizeof(({{ evt_var }}).data) - 1;
            }
            memcpy(({{ evt_var }}).data, _lit, _lit_n);
            ({{ evt_var }}).data[_lit_n] = '\0';
        }
        lua_pop({{ child_L_var }}, 1);
        (void)luaL_dostring({{ child_L_var }}, "_sce_donedata_literal = nil");
    }
{% endmacro %}
